controls.description:
Top management demonstrates leadership and commitment to the AIMS by:
- ensuring the AI policy and AI objectives are set and fit the organization's strategic direction;
- integrating AIMS requirements into business processes;
- making the needed resources available;
- communicating why effective AI management and conformity with the AIMS matter;
- ensuring the AIMS achieves its intended results;
- directing and supporting people to contribute to its effectiveness;
- promoting continual improvement; and
- supporting other managers in showing leadership in their areas.

Top management establishes an AI policy that:
a) is appropriate to the purpose of the organization;
b) provides a framework for setting AI objectives;
c) commits to satisfying applicable requirements;
d) commits to continual improvement of the AIMS.

The policy is documented information, refers to other organizational policies where relevant, is communicated within the organization and is available to interested parties as appropriate.

Top management ensures that responsibilities and authorities for relevant roles are assigned and communicated, including responsibility and authority for:
a) ensuring the AIMS conforms to this standard;
b) reporting on AIMS performance to top management.

Define and establish an AI risk assessment process that:
a) is informed by and aligned with the AI policy and AI objectives;
b) produces consistent, valid and comparable results when repeated;
c) identifies the risks that help or prevent achieving the AI objectives;
d) analyses the AI risks by (1) assessing the potential consequences to the organization, individuals and societies if they materialize, (2) assessing their realistic likelihood where applicable, and (3) determining risk levels;
e) evaluates the AI risks by (1) comparing the analysis results with the risk criteria and (2) prioritizing risks for treatment.

Retain documented information about the AI risk assessment process.

Define an AI risk treatment process, taking the risk assessment results into account, to:
a) select appropriate AI risk treatment options;
b) determine all controls needed to implement the chosen options and compare them with Annex A to verify no necessary control has been omitted;
c) consider the Annex A controls relevant to the treatment options;
d) identify whether controls beyond Annex A are needed;
e) consider the Annex B guidance when implementing the controls from b) and c);
f) produce a statement of applicability listing the necessary controls and the justification for each inclusion and exclusion;
g) formulate an AI risk treatment plan.

Obtain management approval of the treatment plan and acceptance of residual risks, and retain documented information about the AI risk treatment process.

Establish AI objectives at relevant functions and levels. The objectives shall:
a) be consistent with the AI policy;
b) be measurable, where practicable;
c) take into account applicable requirements;
d) be monitored;
e) be communicated;
f) be updated as appropriate;
g) be available as documented information.

When planning how to achieve them, determine what will be done, the resources required, who is responsible, when it will be completed and how results will be evaluated.

The AIMS includes:
a) the documented information this standard requires;
b) the documented information the organization determines is necessary for the AIMS to be effective.

Conduct internal audits at planned intervals to determine whether the AIMS:
a) conforms to (1) the organization's own requirements for its AIMS and (2) the requirements of this standard;
b) is effectively implemented and maintained.

Plan, establish, implement and maintain an audit programme covering frequency, methods, responsibilities, planning requirements and reporting, taking into account the importance of the processes concerned and the results of previous audits. For the programme:
a) define the objectives, criteria and scope of each audit;
b) select auditors and conduct audits so the process is objective and impartial;
c) ensure audit results are reported to the relevant managers.

Retain documented information as evidence of the audit programme and the audit results.

The management review considers:
a) the status of actions from previous reviews;
b) changes in external and internal issues relevant to the AIMS;
c) changes in the needs and expectations of interested parties relevant to the AIMS;
d) information on AIMS performance, including trends in (1) nonconformities and corrective actions, (2) monitoring and measurement results, and (3) audit results;
e) opportunities for continual improvement.

When a nonconformity occurs:
a) react to it and, as applicable, (1) take action to control and correct it and (2) deal with the consequences;
b) evaluate the need for action to eliminate its causes so it does not recur or occur elsewhere, by (1) reviewing it, (2) determining its causes, and (3) determining whether similar nonconformities exist or could occur;
c) implement any action needed;
d) review the effectiveness of corrective action taken;
e) make changes to the AIMS, if necessary.

Corrective actions are appropriate to the effects of the nonconformities. Retain documented information on the nature of the nonconformities, the actions taken and the results of corrective action.
